			<div class="content" id="post-content-3879090">
									

<p>I mostly agree with the points raised by Soixante Huitard, but wanted to add a few things.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Which is why its anomalous. It doesn't make sense, that's the anomaly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's a difference between 'it's paranormal/anomalous' and 'it doesn't make sense'. I could accept 'measuring devices displayed an imaginary number when the element was measured' or 'an 'imaginary' particle was detected in the metal using SCP-X' or 'the metal compels people to refer to the atomic number as imaginary' - all of these rely on something which while obviously not real, could exist in the SCP universe. None of these are necessarily inferred by 'the atomic number is imaginary', though. It's like saying 'the atomic number is potato'. There could be an anomalous element where describing the number of protons in it as 'potato' would be logical, but anyone reading that line in a report without further context would just assume it was nonsense. I only harp on this point because I would actually read an SCP about an element with an imaginary atomic number, while this does a disservice to an interesting idea.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In addition to the Bronze Age/Middle East capitalisation problems, 'iron age' is also not capitalised, and there are a few other grammar problems (I spotted a stray its/it's at least once). While not strictly mistakes, there are a lot of phrasing choices that read oddly to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4000 for the most part, lacked the urbanization</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Such as this - I really feel it would read better as 'Mostly, SCP-4000 lacked&#8230;' or 'SCP-4000, for the most part,&#8230;'. Minor, but this happened enough times to be frustrating by the end.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the nature of SCP-4000, access to knowledge regarding the object has been deemed unnecessary for individuals that are not directly connected with its research or containment process.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I feel like this could apply to literally every SCP.</p>
<p>Describing the military technology as on par with Iron Age kingdoms and then talking about their nuclear weapons seems&#8230; wrong.</p>
<p>Extremely minor, but if that tiny sliver of Syria is enough to describe 'southern Syria' as part of its territory, Jordan should be as well.</p>
<p>My main issue with this, though, is that there are a LOT of SCPs that presuppose the existence of an ancient Foundation. <a href="/scp-557">SCP-557</a> is probably the oldest and the first one I thought of. Without much more fleshing out of the ancient Foundation civilisation, this doesn't bring anything new to the table, in spite of being more wordy.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
